WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.572
Indigenous treaty violations.

00:00:02.522 --> 00:00:04.931
The oldest broken promises.

00:00:04.931 --> 00:00:14.193
Before Confederation, the Crown entered into treaties with Indigenous peoples — legally binding agreements that guaranteed land rights, self-governance, and ongoing obligations.

00:00:14.193 --> 00:00:19.159
These treaties have been systematically violated for over one hundred and fifty years.

00:00:19.159 --> 00:00:26.556
The Truth and Reconciliation Commission issued ninety-four Calls to Action in 2015 — the majority remain incomplete.

00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:36.204
The National Inquiry into Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women and Girls issued two hundred and thirty-one Calls for Justice in 2019 — most remain unimplemented.

00:00:36.204 --> 00:00:40.784
Boil water advisories persist on reserves despite billions allocated.

00:00:40.784 --> 00:00:49.431
Jordan's Principle — the obligation to provide First Nations children with equitable services — has been the subject of ongoing Human Rights Tribunal orders.

00:00:49.431 --> 00:00:55.943
The duty to consult, affirmed by the Supreme Court, is routinely bypassed through inadequate consultation processes.

00:00:55.943 --> 00:00:58.613
This is not historical injustice.

00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:01.795
It is ongoing, documented, and deliberate.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:06.147
Four documented categories of ongoing Indigenous treaty violations.

00:01:06.147 --> 00:01:08.920
The foundational accountability failure.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.886
Indigenous treaty violations are not one category of government failure among many.

00:01:13.886 --> 00:01:18.284
They are the foundational accountability failure that defines the Canadian state.

00:01:18.284 --> 00:01:33.124
If the government can violate legally binding treaties for one hundred and fifty years, ignore its own Truth and Reconciliation Commission, fail to implement the findings of its own National Inquiry, and deny basic services while spending billions — then no accountability mechanism works.

00:01:33.124 --> 00:01:35.431
Not for Indigenous peoples.

00:01:35.431 --> 00:01:37.318
Not for veterans.

00:01:37.318 --> 00:01:39.431
Not for any Canadian.

00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:46.124
The institutional capture documented across this site is the extension of a pattern that began with the first violated treaty.

00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:53.568
The system that fails to honour its obligations to the peoples whose land it occupies cannot be expected to honour its obligations to anyone.
